In article <1990Jun15.153910.1855@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu> bharat@alpha.UUCP (R. Bharat Rao) writes:
>1. What should you #dip your weapon in? I think you drown in pools, so
>   fountains seems the only answer? I know "your sword rusts" is bad,
>   but how do you know if your dipping had a good effect? Should you
>   #dip only once, or keep dipping? Can you dip stuff other than you 
>   sword? Also I know (now) to drop all my stuff before #dipping.

Generally, you don't.  (If you really want to, you can dip it in holy water
to bless it, though other means of blessing exists.)  Dipping stuff in
fountains is mainly useful because 1) a long sword might become Excalibur,
and 2) dipping has a chance of uncursing a cursed item (weapon or not).

>2. After you #loot a chest, should you put something back in? Is there
>   any way to get into these chests other than #force? Does #force
>   have any bad side effects (other than breaking your weapon)?

You don't have to put stuff back in.
You can open the chest by 'a'pplying an appropriately-shaped key, a skeleton
key, a credit card, or a lockpick down at the chest when standing on the same
square.  You may also stand on a square next to the chest and kick (^D) the
chest.
#force has the side effect that you might destroy the box which can break
some of the contents.

>3. How do you figure out at which altar you should #offer? I tried
>   this once and was told that my own lord's power (?) decreased? I
>   mean how do I know which lord is mine?
>4. What does "Chaotic" in my stats mean?

"Chaotic" is your alignment.  "Chaotic" monsters are often friendly, and
"lawful" ones usually aren't.  It also means you should offer at chaotic
altars; the god for those is yours.  (Offering at other altars is not
necessarily bad, as you have a chance of converting the altar to one of yours.)

>5. To feed your dog/cat should you throw food at him, or drop food and
>   walk away, or throw food next to him? Also when trying to train it
>   to steal (I believe this is done by rewarding your pet when it
>   picks some stuff) should you reward it when it picks something up
>   or when it drops it? Should you feed it and then pick the stuff up,
>   or vice versa? Should this be done only at shops or at other times
>   as well? Do you reward your pet for fighting monsters for you?

The "rewarding" actually works when the pet eats non-corpse food, period.
Supposedly it will be more likely to drop stuff next to you then.  This does
not work too well, and you really don't need to worry about it.

>6. What good are statues?

Break them open with a pick-axe and you might find a spellbook inside.

>7. What do those messages like "you hear the footsteps of a guard"
>   mean?

That there is a vault on the level.

>8. What monsters can be safely eaten and are there any side effects?
>   As a barbarian I died from eating a jackal? Go easy on spoilers on
>   this one!

That was not because jackal corpses are unsafe, but because you let the
corpse decay.  Eat corpses when they are fresh.

If you want to find out what's good to eat, try discovery mode (nethack -D).
You can wish up the corpses and try eating them to see what effects you get.
